menu "MicroLink V2 Configuration"

    config ML_ZERO_COPY_WG
        bool "Enable zero-copy WireGuard receive (high-throughput mode)"
        default n
        help
            Use raw lwIP UDP PCB instead of BSD sockets for WireGuard packet
            reception on the DISCO UDP socket. This eliminates memory copies
            and reduces latency by injecting WG packets directly into the
            WireGuard stack from the lwIP tcpip_thread.

            When enabled:
            - WireGuard packets: zero-copy direct injection (no malloc, no memcpy)
            - DISCO packets: lock-free SPSC ring buffer to wg_mgr task
            - STUN packets: still handled via BSD socket + queue

            Enable this for high-throughput applications like video streaming
            (30fps+). For typical IoT/sensor applications (heartbeats, commands),
            the default BSD socket mode is simpler and sufficient.

            Zero-copy mode contributed by dj-oyu (https://github.com/dj-oyu/microlink)

    config ML_MAX_PEERS
        int "Maximum simultaneous WireGuard peers"
        default 16
        range 1 128
        help
            Maximum number of peers with active WireGuard tunnels. Each peer
            uses approximately 200 bytes plus endpoint storage. This is the HARD
            ceiling (peer-table array size); the working set is capped by
            ML_DEFAULT_MAX_PEERS / the runtime max_peers setting.

    config ML_DEFAULT_MAX_PEERS
        int "Default active WireGuard peer set (0 = use ML_MAX_PEERS ceiling)"
        default 32
        range 0 128
        help
            Default working-set size for the active WireGuard peer table when the
            runtime max_peers setting (POST /api/settings) is unset. 32 is the
            production default: it comfortably covers a machine's remote fan-out +
            fleet + operators while keeping DISCO/WG probing churn low on a large
            (100s-of-node) shared tailnet, and stays well under the ML_MAX_PEERS
            ceiling. Pinned peers (configured machines on a remote, operators on a
            machine) are exempt and always kept. Raise toward the ceiling only for
            a machine that must serve more than ~28 remotes. See
            docs/MULTI_REMOTE_MULTI_MACHINE.md.

            Most deployments need 1-4 peers (only the peers you actively
            communicate with). For large tailnets, set this to the actual
            peer count needed, not the total tailnet size.

    config ML_PRIORITY_PEER_IP
        string "Priority peer VPN IP"
        default ""
        help
            Tailscale VPN IP of the priority peer (e.g., "100.x.y.z").
            This peer is guaranteed a WireGuard slot even when the peer table
            is full — the least-recently-used non-priority peer is evicted to
            make room.

            On large tailnets where the NVS cache fills the peer table at boot
            before the priority peer arrives from MapResponse, this ensures
            the peer you care about always gets a slot.

            Can also be set at runtime via the HTTP config server web UI.
            Leave empty to disable (all peers treated equally).

    config ML_NVS_MAX_PEERS
        int "Maximum cached peers in NVS"
        default 128
        range 16 1024
        help
            Maximum number of peers stored in the NVS flash cache. Peers are
            persisted across reboots so DISCO probing can start immediately
            before control plane registration completes.

            Storage is a single packed blob: each peer uses 92 bytes.
            When the cache is full, the least-recently-used peer is evicted.

            Examples:
            - 16:   ~1.5KB NVS blob (small IoT deployments)
            - 64:   ~5.9KB NVS blob (default, most tailnets)
            - 256:  ~23.6KB NVS blob (large tailnets)
            - 1024: ~94.2KB NVS blob (enterprise, requires large NVS partition)

            The blob is PSRAM-backed in memory. Ensure your NVS partition is
            large enough for the configured value.

    config ML_H2_BUFFER_SIZE_KB
        int "HTTP/2 receive buffer size (KB)"
        default 512
        range 16 2048
        help
            Size of the PSRAM-allocated buffer for receiving HTTP/2 frames
            from the Tailscale control plane (MapResponse).

            The initial MapResponse contains all peers in your tailnet.
            Each peer is roughly 1-2KB of JSON. After the initial response,
            delta updates (PeersChanged/PeersRemoved) are much smaller.

            - 64:   ~30 peers (small tailnets)
            - 512:  ~300 peers (default, most deployments)
            - 1024: ~600 peers (large enterprise tailnets)
            - 2048: ~1200 peers (very large tailnets, requires 8MB PSRAM)

    config ML_WG_MGR_STACK_KB
        int "wg_mgr task stack size (KB)"
        default 16
        range 8 32
        help
            Stack for the WG manager task. 16 KB is the safe default: with
            ML_ZERO_COPY_WG off, every direct-UDP WG packet runs the full
            decrypt -> ip_input -> ... -> encrypt chain on this stack, and
            8 KB tripped the canary under sustained ping load (v15.15).

            On no-PSRAM targets (plain ESP32) every KB here comes out of
            the same internal heap that lwIP pbufs and the coord H2 buffer
            need; 12 KB is a measured-risk compromise for light workloads
            (occasional HTTP + heartbeats, QUIET logging).

    config ML_JSON_BUFFER_SIZE_KB
        int "JSON parse buffer size (KB)"
        default 512
        range 16 2048
        help
            Size of the PSRAM-allocated buffer for parsing JSON MapResponse
            data. cJSON builds an in-memory DOM tree that uses 2-3x the
            raw JSON size, so this should be at least as large as the
            HTTP/2 buffer.

            Match this to ML_H2_BUFFER_SIZE_KB unless you have a reason
            to set them differently.



    menu "Credentials"
        comment "Configure via: idf.py menuconfig → MicroLink V2 → Credentials"
        comment "These values are stored in sdkconfig (git-ignored)."

        config ML_WIFI_SSID
            string "WiFi SSID"
            default ""
            help
                SSID of the WiFi network to connect to.
                This is stored in sdkconfig which should be git-ignored.

        config ML_WIFI_PASSWORD
            string "WiFi Password"
            default ""
            help
                Password for the WiFi network.
                This is stored in sdkconfig which should be git-ignored.

        config ML_WIFI_SSID_2
            string "WiFi SSID (secondary)"
            default ""
            help
                Secondary WiFi SSID. When the primary fails to associate,
                the chip cycles through to this one. Used to seed the NVS
                wifi_list on first boot (post-erase-flash) so the device
                tries multiple networks before falling back to SoftAP.

        config ML_WIFI_PASSWORD_2
            string "WiFi Password (secondary)"
            default ""
            help
                Password for the secondary WiFi network.

        config ML_TAILSCALE_AUTH_KEY
            string "Tailscale Auth Key"
            default ""
            help
                Tailscale authentication key from https://login.tailscale.com/admin/settings/keys
                Generate it REUSABLE (the key is re-sent on every re-registration),
                NOT ephemeral (the node identity is persisted in NVS), and
                pre-approved if device approval is on. Disable key expiry on the
                node afterwards, or use a tagged key. See docs/QUICKSTART.md.
                This is stored in sdkconfig which should be git-ignored.

        config ML_DEVICE_TYPE
            string "Device type reported to the management backend"
            default "remote"
            help
                Sent as device_type in every check-in so the backend can
                type-gate firmware assignments (e.g. never offer a remote
                image to a "machine" device). Leave "remote" for pstop
                remotes; machine-role builds set "machine".

        config ML_OTA_REQUIRE_PROJECT
            string "Required project name in accepted OTA images"
            default ""
            help
                If non-empty, an OTA image whose app-descriptor
                project_name differs is rejected before activation —
                device-side backstop against a wrong-lineage rollout
                (e.g. a remote image pushed to a machine). Empty
                disables the check.

        config ML_DEVICE_NAME
            string "Device Name"
            default ""
            help
                Hostname for this device on the Tailscale network.
                Leave empty for auto-generated name (esp32-XXYYZZ from MAC).

    endmenu

    menu "HTTP Config Server"

        config ML_ENABLE_CONFIG_HTTPD
            bool "Enable HTTP configuration server"
            default n
            help
                Enable an HTTP server accessible via the Tailscale VPN IP
                (e.g., http://100.x.x.x/) for runtime configuration of
                WiFi credentials, Tailscale auth key, peer allowlist, etc.

                Settings persist in NVS across reboots. The peer allowlist
                controls which peers receive outbound DISCO probes — on large
                tailnets this prevents wg_mgr flooding and reduces latency.

        config ML_CONFIG_MAX_ALLOWED_PEERS
            int "Maximum peers in allowlist"
            depends on ML_ENABLE_CONFIG_HTTPD
            default 16
            range 1 1024
            help
                Maximum number of peers in the DISCO probe allowlist.
                Each entry uses 28 bytes in NVS (IP + 24-char label).
                ESP-IDF NVS supports multi-page blobs up to 508KB,
                so large allowlists are not a problem.

                - 16:    448 bytes (default, small deployments)
                - 128:   3.6KB
                - 300:   8.4KB (recommended for large fleets)
                - 1024:  28.7KB

                When the allowlist is empty, all peers are probed (backwards
                compatible). When populated, only listed peers receive
                outbound DISCO probes and WG peer table slots.

        config ML_ADMIN_PASSWORD
            string "Admin panel password"
            depends on ML_ENABLE_CONFIG_HTTPD
            default "microlink"
            help
                Password for HTTP Basic Auth on the admin panel (/admin/).
                Username is always "admin". Used by ml_app framework to
                protect configuration, OTA, and monitoring endpoints.

    endmenu

    menu "Managed OTA Updates"

        config ML_FLEET_SERVER_IP
            string "Management server VPN IP"
            default ""
            help
                Tailscale VPN IP of a centralized configuration management
                and OTA server, if you run one (none is included in this
                project). When set, that peer is PERMANENTLY allowed and
                cannot be removed from the runtime allowlist — it must
                always be reachable. Every other peer remains
                user-removable. Leave empty to disable the permanent-allow
                behavior.

        config ML_OTA_BACKEND_URL
            string "OTA backend URL"
            default ""
            help
                Base URL of your OTA backend, if you run one (e.g.
                "https://myapp.example.com"). Leave empty to disable
                OTA polling.

        config ML_OTA_API_KEY
            string "OTA backend API key"
            default ""
            help
                Bearer token for authenticating with the OTA backend.
                Must match the key configured in the backend's config.toml.

        config ML_OTA_CHECK_INTERVAL_S
            int "OTA check-in interval (seconds)"
            default 300
            range 60 300
            help
                How often the unit checks in with the OTA backend (and polls for
                firmware). Capped at 300 (5 minutes) so the backend always hears
                from every unit at least that often; the firmware also clamps
                any persisted/endpoint value to this ceiling. Minimum 60 (1 min).

        config ML_OTA_AUTO_UPDATE
            bool "Enable automatic OTA updates"
            default y
            help
                When enabled, the device automatically downloads and
                applies firmware updates from the backend. Can be
                toggled at runtime via the admin web UI.

    endmenu

    menu "Provisioning AP Fallback"

        config ML_AP_FALLBACK_ENABLE
            bool "Enable SoftAP fallback when no saved WiFi is reachable"
            default y
            help
                If the device cannot join any saved WiFi network within
                ML_AP_FALLBACK_TIMEOUT_S seconds of boot, switch to APSTA
                mode and host its own AP so the user can add a working
                network via the admin panel.

        config ML_AP_FALLBACK_TIMEOUT_S
            int "Seconds without STA connection before AP fallback fires"
            depends on ML_AP_FALLBACK_ENABLE
            default 60
            range 10 600

        config ML_AP_SSID_PREFIX
            string "Prefix for the fallback AP SSID"
            depends on ML_AP_FALLBACK_ENABLE
            default "microlink"
            help
                Full SSID is "<prefix>-XXYYZZ" where XXYYZZ are the last
                three bytes of the device's SoftAP MAC in hex, so each
                unit gets a unique name.

        config ML_AP_PASSWORD
            string "WPA2 password for the fallback AP"
            depends on ML_AP_FALLBACK_ENABLE
            default "microlink"
            help
                Must be at least 8 characters for WPA2. Leave empty to
                make the AP open (no auth) — only do this if you trust
                the local RF environment.

    endmenu

endmenu
